---
layout: layout.njk
title: "Zubra: web components, written like HTML"
description: "Zubra compiles a file of HTML, a script and styles into a standard custom element, the HTML a server sends, and typings. It works in plain HTML, every framework, and on the server."
---
<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap hero-grid">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="kicker">A compiler for web components</p>
      <h1>Write components like HTML. Ship them as the platform’s own.</h1>
      <p class="lede">A Zubra file is a script, some styles and a template. The compiler turns it into a standard custom element, the HTML a server sends, and typings. The element works in plain HTML, in React, Vue, Svelte and the rest, and on the server. Nothing is hidden: you can read every line it writes.</p>
      <div class="install">
        <code id="install-command">npm create zubra@latest</code>
        {{ components.copyButton.html | safe }}
      </div>
      <p class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="{{ '/docs/guide/01-first-component/' | url }}">Start the guide</a>
        <a class="button" href="{{ '/playground/' | url }}">Try the playground</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-code" aria-label="A Zubra component">
      <figcaption>copy-button.zb <span>{{ samples.sourceLines }} lines</span></figcaption>
      {{ samples.source | tighten | highlight("markup") | safe }}
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>
<div class="stripes" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<section class="band" id="output">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Read what it writes</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">That one file compiles to the three things below. This page ran the compiler to produce them, so they are what <code>zubra compile</code> writes today.</p>
    <ol class="pipeline">
      <li class="step">
        <h3><span class="n">1</span> The HTML a server sends</h3>
        <p><code>zubra compile copy-button.zb --props props.json</code></p>
        {{ samples.html | tidy | highlight("markup") | safe }}
        <p class="note">Content on the page before any JavaScript runs. When the script loads, the element binds to this markup in place.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <h3><span class="n">2</span> The element</h3>
        <p><code>zubra compile copy-button.zb --emit js</code></p>
        {{ samples.js | tidy | highlight("javascript") | safe }}
        <p class="note">A description, not a class. One shared definition in the runtime turns it into a custom element.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="step">
        <h3><span class="n">3</span> Typings</h3>
        <p><code>zubra compile copy-button.zb --emit dts</code></p>
        {{ samples.dts | tidy | highlight("javascript") | safe }}
        <p class="note">The element’s properties, events and form-control API, for the editor and for the same file imported into React or Vue.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="more"><a href="{{ '/docs/guide/08-read-the-output/' | url }}">Read one component’s output, rule by rule</a>: why custom elements are awkward by hand, and where each rule shows up in the output.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap by-hand" id="by-hand">
  <h2>Without the compiler</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">This is the same component as a hand-written custom element. It needs shadow DOM to scope one style rule, has no server HTML, and has the two bugs most hand-written elements have: a forgotten <code>#render()</code> and a listener removed with a different reference.</p>
  <div class="versus">
    <div class="side">
      <p class="count"><strong>{{ samples.byHandLines }}</strong> lines by hand</p>
      {{ samples.byHand | highlight("javascript") | safe }}
    </div>
    <div class="side">
      <p class="count"><strong>{{ samples.sourceLines }}</strong> lines with Zubra</p>
      {{ samples.source | tighten | highlight("markup") | safe }}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band" id="anywhere">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Use it where you already are</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">It is a custom element, so anything that renders HTML can use it.</p>
    <div class="uses">
      <div>
        <h3>Plain HTML</h3>
        {{ '<copy-button text="npm create zubra"></copy-button>' | highlight("markup") | safe }}
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>React or Vue</h3>
        {{ '<CopyButton text="npm create zubra" />' | highlight("markup") | safe }}
        <p class="note">Through <code>@zubra/unplugin</code>: the same file, typed.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>On a server</h3>
        {{ 'zubra compile copy-button.zb --props props.json' | highlight("bash") | safe }}
        <p class="note">Or <code>@zubra/server</code> for Express, Fastify, Hono and Eleventy.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="more"><a href="{{ '/docs/who-this-is-for/' | url }}">Who Zubra is for</a>, and <a href="{{ '/docs/what-crosses-the-boundary/' | url }}">what does and doesn’t cross the boundary</a> into a framework.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap" id="beyond">
  <h2>Beyond the browser, if you want it</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">Two packages that sit beside the compiler. A component uses them with one line, and the compiler writes the rest.</p>
  <div class="uses">
    <div>
      <h3>Forms that post to the server</h3>
      {{ "export async function subscribe(formData) {\n  'use server'\n  // runs on the server only\n}\n\n<form action={subscribe}>…</form>" | highlight("javascript") | safe }}
      <p class="note">The function never reaches the browser: its name becomes a URL, and the form posts there with or without JavaScript. <a href="{{ '/docs/actions/' | url }}"><code>@zubra/actions</code></a></p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>State that lives on the device</h3>
      {{ "'use collection notes'\nlet text = ''   // kept in IndexedDB" | highlight("javascript") | safe }}
      <p class="note">Survives a reload, follows across tabs, and shares between devices through a log on any server. <a href="{{ '/docs/sync/' | url }}"><code>@zubra/sync</code></a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="more">Both run in the <a href="{{ '/playground/#example=sync' | url }}">playground</a>, and the guide has a chapter on each.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap start" id="start">
  <h2>Start in a minute</h2>
  <ol class="start-steps">
    <li><code>npm create zubra@latest my-app</code><span>Pick a framework, or none. You get a working app with one component, a dev server that updates components in place, and a browser test.</span></li>
    <li><a href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}">Follow the guide</a><span>Ten short pages: props and state, templates, events, slots, forms, async data, the server, reading the output, server functions, and state on the device.</span></li>
    <li><a href="{{ site.marketplace }}">Install the VS Code extension</a><span>Highlighting for <code>.zb</code> files, the compiler's errors as you type, and completion and hover for your components. <a href="{{ '/docs/editor-support/' | url }}">Other editors work through the language server.</a></span></li>
    <li><a href="{{ '/playground/' | url }}">Or try it in the browser</a><span>The playground compiles in the page and runs your component in plain HTML, React and Vue side by side.</span></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="band" id="demos">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>{{ site.count }} complete apps</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">Every framework, server and kind of client, each tested in a browser on every change. The ones marked “needs a server” run locally: each one’s README has the commands.</p>
    {% for group in site.groups %}
    <h3 class="group">{{ group.name }}</h3>
    <div class="grid">
      {% for demo in group.demos %}
      {% set open = ('/demos/' + demo.id + '/') | url if demo.live else demo.source %}
      <article class="card">
        <a class="shot" href="{{ open }}" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">{% if demo.shot %}<img src="{{ ('/shots/' + demo.id + '.jpg') | url }}" alt="" width="1280" height="800" loading="lazy">{% else %}<span class="placeholder"></span>{% endif %}</a>
        <div class="body">
          <p class="stack">{{ demo.stack }}</p>
          <h4><a href="{{ open }}">{{ demo.title }}</a></h4>
          <p>{{ demo.blurb }}</p>
          <p class="links">{% if demo.live %}<a href="{{ open }}">Open the demo</a>{% else %}<span class="server">Needs a server: run it locally</span>{% endif %} <a href="{{ demo.source }}">Source</a></p>
        </div>
      </article>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
</section>
